  "client_view_text": "  If the log is corrupted in a crash, what happens when the database switches to using the log to recover?\nA write-ahead log (WAL) allows a database to recover after a crash by recording every change made to the database before writing that change to the actual database files. This ensures that the database can be recovered to a consistent state, even if the database crashes in the middle of a transaction. When the database crashes, it can use the log to replay the transactions, applying the recorded changes to the database files and rolling back any uncommitted transactions.\nIf the log is corrupted in a crash, the database may not be able to recover correctly. In this case, the database may fail to start or may produce inconsistent results. The corruption of the log file can occur for a variety of reasons, such as a disk failure or a write error. If the log is corrupted, the database may not be able to determine the correct state of the database, and may attempt to replay transactions that were already committed or roll back transactions that were already completed. This can lead to data loss or corruption, or even cause the database to become stuck in an inconsistent state.\nIf the database is unable to recover from the log, it may resort to other recovery methods, such as a"
